**When to reach for the customizer**
- Property-based config covers 90% of cases (port, SSL, compression, threads)
- Server-specific knobs (Tomcat connector properties, Undertow buffer sizes) need programmatic access
- Per-environment customization that's awkward to express in YAML

**Tomcat-specific tuning**

```java
@Bean
@ConditionalOnClass(name = "org.apache.catalina.startup.Tomcat")
public WebServerFactoryCustomizer<TomcatServletWebServerFactory> tomcatCustomizer() {
    return factory -> factory.addConnectorCustomizers(connector -> {
        connector.setProperty("relaxedQueryChars", "[]");
        connector.setProperty("maxKeepAliveRequests", "200");
        connector.setProperty("connectionTimeout", "20000");
    });
}
```

**Undertow buffer configuration**

```java
@Bean
@ConditionalOnClass(name = "io.undertow.Undertow")
public WebServerFactoryCustomizer<UndertowServletWebServerFactory> undertowCustomizer() {
    return factory -> factory.addBuilderCustomizers(builder ->
        builder.setBufferSize(16 * 1024)
               .setIoThreads(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors())
               .setWorkerThreads(64));
}
```

**Operational notes**
- Customizers only activate when the matching server is on the classpath
- Guard server-specific beans with `@ConditionalOnClass` so the bean isn't loaded after a server switch
- Common production scenario: increase `server.tomcat.threads.max` from 200 only after measuring `tomcat.threads.busy` via `/actuator/metrics`
- Move slow operations to `@Async` thread pools before scaling Tomcat threads — frees request threads quickly
